From: Alan Sun <shou.shih@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] spi: do not fail the build when SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD is absent

SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD is a vendor-kernel flag that does not exist
upstream. The #else branch raises a #warning, and ccflags-y carries -Werror, so
it is fatal rather than advisory and the driver does not build at all:

  spi.c:1562:2: error: #warning "SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD macro not
  defined" [-Werror=cpp]
  cc1: all warnings being treated as errors

This is not limited to mainline. raspberrypi/linux 6.6.51+rpt-rpi-v8 does not
define the macro either, so the released driver cannot be built against a
current Raspberry Pi OS without editing the source first.

Nothing needs to be done in that branch. A kernel without the flag already
forces SPI_CS_HIGH for a cs-gpios device and lets gpiolib apply the active-low
inversion exactly once, which is the behaviour the flag existed to obtain.
Replace the #warning with a comment saying so.

---
diff --git a/spi.c b/spi.c
index e5facbd..31559da 100644
--- a/spi.c
+++ b/spi.c
@@ -1516,7 +1516,18 @@ static int morse_spi_probe(struct spi_device *spi)
 #ifdef SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD
 	spi->controller->flags |= SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD;
 #else
-#warning "SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD macro not defined"
+	/*
+	 * SPI_CONTROLLER_ENABLE_CS_GPIOD is a vendor-kernel flag and does not
+	 * exist upstream. Kernels without it already force SPI_CS_HIGH for a
+	 * cs-gpios device and let gpiolib apply the active-low inversion once,
+	 * which is the behaviour the flag was there to obtain, so there is
+	 * nothing to do here.
+	 *
+	 * This was a #warning, and ccflags-y carries -Werror, so it was fatal
+	 * rather than advisory: the driver did not build at all on such a
+	 * kernel -- including raspberrypi/linux 6.6.51, which does not define
+	 * the macro either.
+	 */
 #endif
 #endif
 	morse_spi_xfer_init(mspi);
